.TH RCPU 1
.SH NAME
rcpu, rimport \- run a command on, or mount a tree of, a 9front machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rcpu
[
.B -k
.I keypattern
]
[
.B -u
.I user
]
[
.B -h
.I host
]
[
.B -d
.I dir
]
[
.B -c
.I cmd
.RI [ arg ...]
]
.PP
.B rimport
[
.B -abc
]
[
.B -k
.I keypattern
]
[
.B -u
.I user
]
.I host
.I tree
.I mountpoint
.SH DESCRIPTION
These speak to a 9front cpu server as 9front's own
.I rcpu
and
.I rimport
do, through its
.B rcpu
service (port 17019): 9front's authentication,
.B p9any
with
.B dp9ik
(see
.IR factotum (4)),
then TLS with the session secret as a pre-shared key, then an
.I rc
script the server runs with the connection as its input and output.
They need a
.B dp9ik
key in
.IR factotum (4)
for the server's authentication domain, for instance
.IP
.EX
echo 'key proto=dp9ik dom=9front user=glenda !password=...' \e
	'auth=tcp!authhost!567' >/mnt/factotum/ctl
.EE
.PP
.I Host
is a name or address
(network
.B tcp
and port 17019 when not given).
.B -k
adds attributes to the key's query and
.B -u
names the user.
.PP
.I Rimport
has 9front's
.I exportfs
serve
.I tree
over the connection and mounts it on
.IR mountpoint ,
replacing what is there, or after
.RB ( -a )
or before
.RB ( -b )
it, with
.B -c
to allow creation.
.PP
.I Rcpu
runs
.I cmd
with its arguments, or an interactive
.B rc
when there is none, on the server, as the key's user, in directory
.I dir
if given (with
.B -d ,
else the user's home), with
.B $service
set to
.BR cpu .
As 9front's
.I rcpu
does, the server mounts what this end serves over the same connection at
.BR /mnt/term ,
and the command's input, output and error are
.BR /mnt/term/fd/10 ,
.B 11
and
.BR 12 ,
which are this command's;
.B /mnt/term/dev/cons
is bound over its
.BR /dev/cons .
9front's
.I rcpu
serves its whole name space there.
This serves a tree of its own with those files in it and nothing else of this machine:
.B fd/10 fd/11 fd/12
.BR dev/cons ,
.BR mnt/cpunote/data ,
from which no note is ever read (interrupts are not passed on), and
.BR env/rstatus ,
where the server leaves the command's exit status, which becomes
.IR rcpu 's.
.PP
Both are refused in CNSA mode: 9front's authentication and its TLS are
classical cryptography (Ed448, ChaCha20-Poly1305, SHA-256), and this is
for working with 9front, not a part of CNSA 2.0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Mount a 9front file server's root and run a command there:
.IP
.EX
rimport cirno / /n/cirno
ls /n/cirno/usr/glenda
rcpu -h cirno -c 'cat /dev/sysname; ps | wc -l'
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/rcpu.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/rimport.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/rconnect.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cpu (1),
.IR dp9ik (2),
.IR factotum (4),
.IR import (4)
.SH BUGS
Notes (an interrupt) are not sent to the far end, and 9front's
.B -p
(the
.I aan
filter, which keeps a session across a dropped connection) is not offered.
There is no drawterm: a 9front program has no
.B /dev/draw
of this machine's in
.BR /mnt/term .
